===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To take on a supply of coal (usually of steam ships or locomotives).
#* '''1863''', Colonial Secretary to Commander Baldwin, USN
#*: shortly after that she '''coaled''' again at Simon's Bay; and that after remaining in the neighbourhood of our ports for a time, she proceeded to Mauritius, where she '''coaled''' again, and then returned to this colony.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1887|author=Harriet W. Daly|title=Digging, Squatting, and Pioneering Life in the Northern Territory of South Australia|page=131|passage=Our next stopping-place was Newcastle, and here we '''coaled''' in earnest, for the steamer was flying light, and was loaded up in every available place.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1890|author=w:Oscar Wilde|title=The Picture of Dorian Gray|chapter=XVI|passage=The light shook and splintered in the puddles. A red glare came from an outward-bound steamer that was '''coaling'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1949|month=November and December|journal=Railway Magazine|page=371 (photo caption)|text=N.W.R. four-cylinder 4-6-2 class "XS1," No. 761, '''coaling''' at Delhi junction. This class is the most powerful passenger engine in India.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To supply with coal.
#: {{ux|en|to '''coal''' a steamer}}
#* '''January 1917''', ''National Geographic Magazine'', Volume 31 Number 1, ''One Hundred British Seaports''
#*: Cruisers may be '''coaled''' at sea and provided with ammunition openly. The submarine may not
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1944|month=January and February|author=W. McGowan Gradon|title=Forres as a Railway Centre|journal=Railway Magazine|page=23|text=After working the 1.30 p.m. through train from Forres to Aberdeen as far as Elgin, she returns tender first with a local passenger train and is then '''coaled''' and watered at Forres shed, and eventually works back to Perth on the 10.20 p.m. through freight.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be converted to [[charcoal]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2014|author=Ken Mudge; Steve Gabriel|title=Farming the Woods
|passage=After the initial burn the goal of any good fire should be '''coaling'''; that is, creating a bed of solid coals that will sustain the fire.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1957|author=H.R. Schubert|title=History of the British Iron and Steel Industry|page=18|passage=As a result, particles of wood and twigs insufficiently '''coaled''' are frequently found at the bottom of such pits.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To burn to charcoal; to [[char]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1622|author=Francis Bacon|title=Natural History
|passage=Char-coal of roots, '''coaled''' into great pieces.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[mark]] or [[delineate]] with charcoal.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1551|author=William Camden|title=Remains concerning Britain|passage={{...}} marvailing, he '''coaled''' out these rithms upon the wall near to the picture}}

